@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.Head
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.LearnPage
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.SiteUrl
@param page: LearnPage
@param head: Head
@param site: SiteUrl

<%--
    /learn/hotswap. NO BACKTICKS anywhere in this file -- one closes the raw string this
    page is written in, and jte then reports the error on the wrong line.

    SOURCES, checked 2026-10-03:
      - Kailh's MX hotswap socket (CPG151101S11) is a surface-mount part: LCSC's listing of
        Kailh's datasheet.
      - Mill-Max: Keebio's guide "How to make your keyboard hotswappable with Mill-Max
        sockets" (blog.keeb.io, linked from the page; rechecked 2026-10-04): the sockets
        are soldered into the switch holes from the underside, after
        being set on the switch's legs; "Mill-Max sockets can fit onto Choc switches,
        however, most PCBs designed for Chocs have switch pad holes that are too small for
        the sockets to fit into them."
      - What a design takes is read off its own pads by scripts/measure_switch_pads.py
        (domain SwitchMount), never off footprint names, so "solder only" is a measured
        answer and an unread board is its own count.
    The diagram is a cross-section sketch, NOT to scale.
--%>
@template.layout(head = head, site = site, content = @`
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.articleLd}</script>
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.breadcrumbLd}</script>
    <article class="learn">
        <p class="crumb"><a href="/learn">&larr; Learn</a></p>
        <h1>Hotswap sockets, Mill-Max or solder</h1>

        <p class="learn-answer">
            A hotswap keyboard lets you push switches in and pull them out without
            soldering. It does that with sockets, either Kailh hotswap sockets or Mill-Max
            sockets. A board with neither needs every switch soldered in.
        </p>

        <figure class="learn-figure">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 150 48" role="img"
                 aria-label="Three side views of a switch on a PCB. Soldered: the switch's two pins pass through the board and are soldered underneath. Kailh hotswap socket: a socket is soldered to the underside of the board and the switch's pins push into it. Mill-Max: a metal sleeve is soldered into each hole and the switch's pins push into the sleeves.">
                <rect class="lf-part" x="13" y="12" width="18" height="10" rx="1"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="19" y="8" width="6" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="12" y="22" width="20" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-board" x="4" y="26" width="36" height="4"/>
                <path class="lf-line" d="M17 26V32.5"/>
                <path class="lf-mark" d="M15.4 30H18.6L17.3 32.2H16.7Z"/>
                <path class="lf-line" d="M26 26V32.5"/>
                <path class="lf-mark" d="M24.4 30H27.6L26.3 32.2H25.7Z"/>
                <text class="lf-label" x="22" y="40" text-anchor="middle">Soldered</text>
                <text class="lf-note" x="22" y="44.5" text-anchor="middle">pins soldered to the board</text>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="63" y="12" width="18" height="10" rx="1"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="69" y="8" width="6" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="62" y="22" width="20" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-board" x="54" y="26" width="36" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-sock" x="64" y="30" width="16" height="3.4" rx="0.5"/>
                <rect class="lf-mark" x="61.5" y="30" width="2.5" height="1.2"/>
                <rect class="lf-mark" x="80" y="30" width="2.5" height="1.2"/>
                <path class="lf-line" d="M67 26V32.4"/>
                <path class="lf-line" d="M76 26V32.4"/>
                <text class="lf-label" x="72" y="40" text-anchor="middle">Kailh hotswap socket</text>
                <text class="lf-note" x="72" y="44.5" text-anchor="middle">socket soldered under the board</text>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="113" y="12" width="18" height="10" rx="1"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="119" y="8" width="6" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="112" y="22" width="20" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-board" x="104" y="26" width="36" height="4"/>
                <rect class="lf-sock" x="115.9" y="25" width="2.2" height="6.5" rx="0.3"/>
                <path class="lf-mark" d="M115.2 30H118.8L118.1 31.6H115.9Z"/>
                <path class="lf-line" d="M117 26V30.5"/>
                <rect class="lf-sock" x="124.9" y="25" width="2.2" height="6.5" rx="0.3"/>
                <path class="lf-mark" d="M124.2 30H127.8L127.1 31.6H124.9Z"/>
                <path class="lf-line" d="M126 26V30.5"/>
                <text class="lf-label" x="122" y="40" text-anchor="middle">Mill-Max sockets</text>
                <text class="lf-note" x="122" y="44.5" text-anchor="middle">one soldered into each hole</text>
            </svg>
            <figcaption>
                Not to scale. Solder is in the accent color, sockets are outlined in it.
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Kailh hotswap sockets</strong> are small plastic parts with two metal
                contacts, soldered to pads under the board. The switch pins go through the
                holes and into the socket. There is one shape for MX and one for Choc, and
                ${page.count("bothSockets")} designs have pads for both. Gateron also makes one for MX.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Mill-Max sockets</strong> are tiny metal sleeves that go in the switch
                holes. <a href="https://blog.keeb.io/how-to-make-your-keyboard-hotswappable-with-mill-max-sockets/">Keebio's guide</a>: put one on each leg of a switch, push it into the board,
                solder the sleeves from underneath. After that the switch pulls out and the
                sleeves stay. Keebio says most Choc boards have holes too small for them.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Soldered</strong> is the simplest board with the fewest parts. Some
                boards take either a socket or solder, and the Hotswap line on the design
                page says so.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            From each board's pads:
            <a class="learn-all" href="/designs?hotswap=kailh">${page.count("kailh")} designs</a> take Kailh sockets,
            <a class="learn-all" href="/designs?hotswap=gateron">${page.count("gateron")} designs</a> Gateron's,
            <a class="learn-all" href="/designs?hotswap=millmax">${page.count("millmax")} designs</a> Mill-Max, and
            <a class="learn-all" href="/designs?hotswap=no">${page.count("no")} designs</a> are solder only.
            For ${page.count("unread")} designs the pads haven't been read yet.
        </p>
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("kailh"))
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("millmax"))
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("solder"))
    </article>
`)
